#! mathcas 2 notebook

#% title
# weierstrass_p

#% text
# `weierstrass_p(u, [g2, g3])` gives the Weierstrass elliptic function `p(u; g2, g3)`.

#% section
# Details

#% item
# `p` is the even elliptic function with a double pole `1/u^2` at the lattice points that solves `p'^2 = 4 p^3 - g2 p - g3` (DLMF 23.2, 23.3).

#% item
# Numerical values come from the roots of `4 t^3 - g2 t - g3`, half-periods from the complete integrals `K(m)` (by the arithmetic-geometric mean) and the theta series of `theta_1` at the reduced argument (DLMF 23.6).

#% item
# `weierstrass_p(u, [0, 0]) = 1/u^2`; the value at `u = 0` is `complex_infinity`; the function is even.

#% section
# Examples

#% subsection
# Basic examples

#% cell
weierstrass_p(5.0, [1, 2])
#% output
#= 18.350508193585224

#% cell
n(weierstrass_p(1/3, [1, 2]), 30)
#% output
#= 9.0064386881227981952859598594593

#% cell
weierstrass_p(2.0 + im, [im, im + 2])
#% output
#= -0.44149302509780536 + 0.5836125857258407im

#% cell
weierstrass_p([1.0, 2.0], [1, 2])
#% output
#= [1.1236536562011785, 2.6585414738960105]

#% cell
weierstrass_p(-x, [g2, g3])
#% output
#= weierstrass_p(x, [g2, g3])

#% text
# The differential equation:

#% cell
p = weierstrass_p(0.7, [1, 2]); weierstrass_p_prime(0.7, [1, 2])^2 - (4*p^3 - p - 2)
#% output
#= 0.0

